# Angular PDF Editor Development Rules

You are a senior Angular engineer working on PDFForge's web editor.

Build a modern, maintainable Angular 21 application with a thin UI layer over the Rust PDF engine.

Phase 3 only — do not scaffold Angular until the backend phase is accepted.

## Core Rules

- Use Angular 21+ modern APIs and syntax.
- Use standalone components. Do not set `standalone: true` in decorators.
- Prefer signals for local/component state.
- Do not introduce NgRx or another global state-management framework.
- Prefer Angular `resource` / `httpResource` patterns where appropriate.
- Keep components focused. Avoid large "god components". Keep files preferably below 300 lines.
- Use TypeScript strict mode. Avoid `any`. Prefer strong domain types.
- Use `inject()` and `OnPush`. Avoid unnecessary subscriptions.
- Use native `@if` / `@for` / `@switch`. Do not use `ngClass` or `ngStyle`.

## UI Architecture

```text
web/src/app/
  core/
  shared/
  features/
    editor/
    documents/
    pages/
```

Organize around features. Isolate PDF editor UI from generic components.

## PDF Editor Rules

Keep rendering, document state, selection, editing commands, and UI state separate.

Do not parse or modify PDF internals in Angular. Talk to the Rust API through typed contracts.

Do not use pdf.js `TextLayer` for editing.

## State

Use signals for selected page, zoom, selected object, active tool, editor state, UI panels, and pending edits.

Do not store large PDF binaries in reactive state. Use blob URLs.

## Styling

Use SCSS. Prefer BEM. Avoid `!important`, deeply nested selectors, and inline styles unless dynamically required.

## Accessibility

Interactive controls need keyboard access, labels, visible focus, and semantic HTML.

## Completion Criteria

TypeScript compile, lint, and tests pass. No unnecessary `any`. No new global state library. Components stay focused. No `!important`.
